# Line endings: LF in the repository, and LF in the working tree too.
#
# The repository already stored LF — `core.autocrlf` handled that — but on
# Windows that same setting checks every file *out* with CRLF, and Prettier
# defaults to `endOfLine: 'lf'`. So `prettier --check`, which `pnpm lint` and
# `pnpm verify` both run, failed on all 83 source files the moment a branch
# switch rewrote the working tree. CI never saw it: CI checks out on Linux.
#
# Added 2026-08-27, after exactly that happened on the merge of Week 2 and left
# `pnpm verify` red on a tree with no actual changes in it. `eol=lf` makes the
# checkout match what the tooling expects, so the trap does not come back on
# the next branch switch.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Doc 12 §3's self-hosted font subsets. `binary` is shorthand for
# `-text -diff`, so git neither converts nor tries to diff them. (The music and
# video fixtures are binary as well, and git tells so from their bytes.)
*.woff2 binary

# Subtitle fixtures are bytes as a reader's disk holds them, line endings
# included: the SubRip one is CRLF with a byte-order mark on purpose, and
# `subtitles.ts` has to read past both (doc 09 §3). Normalising it to LF would
# quietly test the easy case.
src/lib/widgets/media/__fixtures__/*.srt -text
src/lib/widgets/media/__fixtures__/*.vtt -text
